\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The half-turn commutant in sector bases}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For each sign, choose the coefficient map
#{C_\varepsilon:V_\varepsilon\to\mathbb R^2} in the ordered basis
#{(b_{\varepsilon,0},b_{\varepsilon,1})} of the
[half-turn sectors](fdip-000L). Their direct sum gives
#{C:V\to\mathbb R^2\oplus\mathbb R^2}. The restriction
isomorphism of \ref{fdip-003T} becomes
##{\operatorname{Comm}(P)\cong M_2(\mathbb R)\times M_2(\mathbb R).}
In these coordinates #{P=\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2)}. For
#{R=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D\end{pmatrix}}, its commuting condition is
##{RP=PR\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad 2B=2C=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad B=C=0.}
The group-algebra image is
#{\{\operatorname{diag}(xI_2,yI_2):x,y\in\mathbb R\}},
with #{x=a+b}, #{y=a-b}, #{a=(x+y)/2} and #{b=(x-y)/2}.
The swapping witness in the proof has array
#{\operatorname{diag}\left(\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},0\right)}.
These formulas realize the intrinsic restriction and strict inclusion,
using the convention of
\citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations}.}
